# The `squash-stack` tool (design/stacks.md, "Publishing"): the std/go worker
# image with this directory's program curried onto it, plus the `help` it is
# described by. A writer run on the conversation: `@in` hands it the
# conversation tree, and it proposes that tree with the plan's `into` replaced.
HELP=<<END
Squash a stack to one commit per layer, for publishing each layer as a branch
with a linear history, as a plan file says. The layers keep their own merge
history; this only mints new commits beside them.

The plan is one key=value per line:

  onto=mystack/00-base
  into=publish/mystack
  layer=01-feature
  commit=mystack/01-feature
  message=Add the feature
  message=
  message=Why, in a body.
  layer=02-tests
  commit=mystack/02-tests
  message=Test the feature

onto and into come once, before the first layer=. onto and each commit are a
conversation path to a gitlink or a full commit hash. Each layer= starts a block
that becomes <into>/<layer>, with one commit= and its message= lines, which in
order are that layer's commit message.

For each layer in order, C = commit(tree = the commit's tree, parent = the
previous C, or onto for the first layer, message = its message lines), with the
author and committer copied from the commit. The same plan always mints the same
commits. They are written to into as one folder of gitlinks, replacing what a
previous run wrote there. Refused, naming both, unless each layer's commit
contains the one before it (or onto): merge a changed lower layer up first.

Then publish_source each <into>/<layer>, bottom to top, with rewrite=true.
@param plan Conversation path of the plan file, e.g. `mystack.plan`.
@writer
@in
END
curry --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/go --worker1:@=worker.go --help=$HELP
